AD  - Specializes in Ancient Pharmacy and Medicine, Madison, WI, United States
AB  - The origins of ancient Mediterranean pharmacy and the role of snake-derived venoms in pre-Hippocratic medicine are poorly understood. An analysis of ancient Greek and Latin written sources within the context of the native technical vocabularies employed by ancient priestesses and physicians reveals the existence and practice of a sophisticated polyvalent pharmacy in which human health was maintained and modified by the administration of viperid venoms and “antidotes” developed in the bodies of priestesses and initiates. Medico-religious practices, involving the use of venoms and their antidotes are traceable to a single late Bronze Age priestess, a Colchian named Medea, whose pharmaceutical experimentation with venoms, toxic botanicals, fungi, and antidotes informed both pagan and Christian mystery religions and established the Western religious concept of communion. © 2019 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
